  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 310 AM EDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nAs the positively tilted trough amplifies and digs \nacross the eastern half of the United States, a strengthening \nsurface low will strengthen and push east-northeastward in \ntandem. A sprawling frontal boundary attached to the low is\nforecast to push across the southeastern United States on Friday\nbefore arriving across the region during the upcoming weekend. As\nsurface ridging slides further to the east, winds across South\nFlorida will begin to veer to a southeasterly component. This will\nusher in even deeper atmospheric and continue the trend of 1)\nhigher rain chances and 2) higher apparent temperatures. Friday\ncould very well be the warmest day of the week with feels-like\ntemperatures once again reaching the 100-105F range across South\nFlorida. The breakdown of stronger background flow across the \nregion will result in sea-breeze circulations being the driving \nmechanism of where afternoon convection focuses, which may reside\nmainly across inland areas and Lake Okeechobee as both sea-breezes\nconverge inland. \n\nThe placement of the aforementioned frontal boundary in relation to \nSouth Florida, surface high pressure axis to the north, and any \nmesoscale coastal convergence will have a direct influence on \npotential rainfall totals, wind gusts, and the severity of any \nmarine/coastal impacts across the region during the upcoming weekend \ninto next week. Forecasted precipitable water and low level RH \nvalues (via forecast soundings) during this time period appear to \napproach the daily max for this time of year, indicative of an \nairmass that is saturated in the low level boundary layer. 500mb \ntemperatures are also forecast to be on the warmer side which may \nsupport an expansive warm cloud layer and heavy rainfall. The \nWeather Prediction Center has placed portions of the east coast of\nSouth Florida in a Day 4 and Day 5 Marginal risk of excessive \nrainfall (Level 1 of 4). In this type of complex weather regime, \nit is far too early to discuss specific rainfall totals as\nanchoring bias to specific values could hurt messaging more than\nhelp it. The main takeaway is that forecast confidence is\nincreasing that heavy rainfall could occur across portions of\nSouth Florida this weekend into early next week. Outside of any \nheavy rain threat, guidance has also been consistent in showing \nthe enhancement of a pressure gradient across the western Atlantic\nand South Florida. This would act to increase wind speeds across \nthe area, resulting in gusty conditions and potential marine and \ncoastal impacts. While forecast confidence has increased in a wet\nand windy weekend, uncertainty as far as timing and impacts\nremain. Stay tuned for forecast updates as we progress through the\nremainder of the work week. An increase in wind and rain chances \nmay keep ambient and apparent temperatures lower beginning during \nthe upcoming weekend.",
